From 3bfd5b07ac356a70dda10d96333e90c06750608b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 06:57:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [DVBridge] Low-Latency: the GUI composited by the packer as
 it packs, the same bytes

In Dolby Vision Low-Latency the bridge maps the picture to the display into a 3840x2160
RGBA32F texture, CompositeGui() blends Kodi's GUI into that texture, and the packer of the
converted transport reads it back. A frame with a GUI therefore read and wrote 32 bytes a pixel
under the GUI in a 128-bit float target, with blending, and a picture that does not fill the
raster had the whole texture (132 MB) cleared again on the next frame, because the GUI had been
drawn over its bars. CroqueMr's CB1 (#197) folds the GUI into its Low-Latency reconstruction
instead, blending in display-linear light, which changes the picture under a translucent GUI.
Hades already composites the GUI exactly in the packer of the source transport (dvguiinplace,
PACK_GUI); this does the same for Low-Latency.

The packer of the converted transport gets a second program: the same shader with
DVBRIDGE_PACK_GUI, which samples the GUI texture where the composite samples it and does the
composite's arithmetic (KODI_GUI_EXACT, PQ in full range, the GUI's tone curve and white, the
coverage squared, then colour a + picture (1 - a)) before it packs. With Low-Latency a frame
asks for it wherever the composite converts exactly and the packer has the program, in every
frame (Low-Latency sends no metadata: there is no level 5 block to measure). The caller then
skips CompositeGui(); PQ subtitles and captures give the composite back
(dvbridge_render_complete()). A pack that fails is not shown and the packer never packs so
again. kodi.log says once that it is used, that it is not made on a GPU, or that it failed.

The program is made only where fragment shaders have quad operations: there each pixel
composites the GUI once and the odd pixel of a pair takes its even pixel's colour from the
quad, and every shortcut (no GUI, a black or an opaque texel) is taken only where the whole
quad takes it, as composited_quad() of the source transport does. With each pixel taking its
own way, a helper invocation left the picture out of the value it swapped: 13 odd pixels came
out up to 59 codes off on the software bridge. Without quad operations the odd pixel would
composite its even pixel's GUI again; that form gives the same bytes, but it measured slower
than the composite where the GUI stays still (24.1 against 17.5 ms with a seek bar over a tenth
of the raster, 28.5 against 23.0 ms with the player information over 40 %), so it is not made.
Every GPU in the logs of Hades' users packs with quad operations.

Measured on the software bridge (zink on lavapipe, picture 51 of the profile 7 FEL test stream,
Kodi's own composite shaders, 21 repetitions, median GPU time of the composite and the packing):
a GUI over 10 % of the raster 33.2 ms over the whole quad and 15.5 ms in its tiles, now 18.4; over
40 %, 32.9 and 21.4, now 20.2; over all of it, 33.1, now 22.7; the 6.9 ms clear of a picture
that does not fill the raster is gone. The transports against the composite and the packing,
byte for byte: 0 pixels differ with any of the three GUIs, and a frame without a GUI is packed
as before; mutations of the coverage, of the GUI's row and of the quad form are caught. The
software bridge does not show what a GPU saves most: the read and the blended write of 32 bytes
a pixel under the GUI.

<video><dvguiinplace>false</dvguiinplace></video> in advancedsettings.xml turns it off, as it
does for the source transport.
